<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="formaldehyde oxidation VII (THF pathway)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-7909" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The detoxification of the highly toxic |FRAME: FORMALDEHYDE| is a major biochemical necessity for most life forms. In the case of methylotrophic bacteria, formaldehyde is not just a toxic compound, but also a central intermediate. It has been suggested that the formaldehyde concentration in the cytoplasm of a methylotroph would rise to 100mM in less than 1 minute if formaldehyde consumption would stop |CITS: [11073907]|. Several different pathways for |FRAME: Formaldehyde-Oxidation| are known in bacteria, encoded by unrelated or distantly related genes. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Different variations of this pathway are known - one utilizes the cofactor |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate| (H&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;F), while the other uses the &quot;archaea-like&quot; cofactor |FRAME: THMPT| (H&lt;sub&gt;4&lt;/sub&gt;MPT). The reaction of formaledehyde with |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate| is spontaneous |CITS: [5954363][14645276]|, but the condensation of formaldehyde with |FRAME: THMPT| is catalyzed by an enzyme (|FRAME: EC-4.2.1.147|). It has been shown that in some well-studied methylotrophs such as |FRAME: TAX-272630| the different variants of the pathway run in opposite directions and serve very different physiological roles. The |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate|-based pathway is a major biosynthetic pathway (|FRAME: PWY-1722|), while the |FRAME: THMPT|-based pathway is used mostly for the generation of reducing power (|FRAME: PWY-1723|). However, a |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate|-based formaldehyde oxidation pathway has been reported in the facultative methylotroph |FRAME: TAX-1471| |CITS: [26303953]|. During methylotrophic growth the organism produces large amounts of |FRAME: FORMALDEHYDE| that must be processed. The presence of a linear formaldehyde oxidation pathway that includes formaldehyde dehydrogenase and formate dehydrogenase in |FRAME: TAX-1471| has been reported already in 2002, although the nature of the cofactor involved was not known at the time |CITS: [12368456]|. Surprisingly, bioinformatic analysis of the genome suggested that the pathway uses the |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate| cofactor |CITS: [22610424]|. Experiments using &lt;sup&gt;13&lt;/sup&gt;C labeled formaldehyde have confirmed that exposure to fomaldehyde results in the appearance of |FRAME: FORMYL-THF-GLU-N &quot;formyltetrahydrofolate|. THF usually carries several glutamate residues, with the exact numbers depending on the species. In the case of |FRAME: TAX-1471|, only |FRAME: CPD-1301| and |FRAME: CPD-12995| versions of |FRAME: THF-GLU-N tetrahydrofolate| are used in formaldehyde oxidation |CITS: [26303953]|. Including this pathway, four different formaldehyde-metabolizing pathways operate in |FRAME: TAX-1471|: one assimilatory pathway (|FRAME: PWY-1861|) and three dissimilatory pathways - |FRAME: RUMP-PWY|, |FRAME: PWY-7909|, and |FRAME: PWY-7908| |CITS: [26303953]|.</Description>
